# What must never enter the build context.
#
# Two entries here are security, not tidiness, and they are the reason this file
# exists before the Dockerfile does:
#
#   .env*   holds a live SMTP App Password. Anything copied into an image stays
#           in that layer forever, readable by anyone who can pull it — even if
#           a later layer deletes the file.
#   data/   holds session id hashes, sealed outbox payloads, and the approval
#           token table. Baking a snapshot of those into an image would ship
#           somebody's live credentials with the application.
#
# Both are mounted at run time instead: secrets through the environment, state
# through a volume.

# Secrets and local configuration
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.bak-*

# Runtime state — mounted as a volume, never copied
data/

# Build output and dependencies. node_modules is rebuilt inside the image from
# the lockfile; copying the host's would import platform-specific binaries and
# whatever has drifted since the last install.
node_modules/
.next/
.next-*/
out/
build/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Version control and CI
.git/
.github/
.gitignore

# Editor and OS noise
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Test and coverage artefacts
coverage/

# Documentation that does not affect the build
README.md
AGENTS.md
CLAUDE.md

# The Docker files themselves
Dockerfile*
docker-compose*.yml
.dockerignore
